<h2>Anatomy of the scam</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. The hook.</strong> A call or message about a parcel with contraband, a SIM issued in the victim\'s name used in crime, or an account "linked to money laundering". The caller cites real-sounding FIR numbers and case IDs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. The escalation.</strong> The call is transferred to a fake "senior officer" on video — uniforms, office backdrops, forged identity cards and "warrants" bearing agency logos.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The confinement.</strong> The victim is told they are under "digital arrest": they must stay on camera, tell no one, and skip work — manufactured isolation that defeats the natural instinct to consult family or a lawyer.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The extraction.</strong> Money is demanded for "verification", "RBI clearance" or "refundable security", transferred by RTGS or UPI to mule accounts and layered onward within hours.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the premise collapses on contact with law</h2>
<p>Every step of the script contradicts the actual law of arrest and investigation. Under the BNSS, an arrested person must be informed of the grounds of arrest, has the right to have a relative or friend informed, and must be produced before a magistrate within twenty-four hours — in person, not by video call controlled by the arresting officer. Police verification never involves transferring funds; no agency maintains "safe accounts" for parking a suspect\'s money; and summons and notices from real agencies arrive through verifiable channels and never direct payment. A genuine officer\'s identity can be verified by calling the office concerned through its publicly listed numbers — something the script forbids precisely because it is fatal to the fraud.</p>

<h2>The offence stack</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Conduct in the scam</th><th>Provision engaged</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Inducing transfer of money by deception</td><td>Section 318 BNS — cheating (formerly s.420 IPC)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pretending to be a police/ED/customs officer</td><td>Section 319 BNS — cheating by personation; Section 204 BNS — personating a public servant</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Threat of arrest to extract money</td><td>Section 308 BNS — extortion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fake warrants, ID cards, seizure memos</td><td>Sections 336 and 340 BNS — forgery and using forged documents/electronic records</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Use of another\'s identity and computer-based personation</td><td>Sections 66C and 66D, Information Technology Act, 2000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coordinated racket with mule-account networks</td><td>Section 111 BNS — organised crime, in appropriate cases</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>The first forty-eight hours</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Immediately:</strong> end the call and stop all further transfers; dial 1930 and report every transaction with UTR numbers; lodge the complaint on cybercrime.gov.in and save the acknowledgement number; instruct the bank in writing to flag and attempt recall of the transfers. <strong>Then:</strong> pursue registration of an FIR with the local cyber police station — in Delhi, each district has a cyber police station, and the complaint portal routes to them; preserve the device, call logs, numbers, screenshots and any documents the fraudsters sent; and do not engage "recovery agents" who promise to retrieve funds for a fee — a common second-round scam targeting the same victims.</p>
</div>

<h2>For families and employers</h2>
<p>The scam\'s engine is isolation under authority. The single most protective habit is also the simplest: no genuine agency objects to a person saying they will consult a lawyer and appear through proper channels. A family member who finds someone sequestered on a long video call with "officers" should interrupt it; a bank employee who sees an anxious customer transferring large sums while on a call is often the last realistic line of defence, and the RBI and banks have standing advisories encouraging exactly that intervention. Victims should also remember that being deceived is not an offence — hesitation born of embarrassment is what gives the layered transfers time to disappear.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Practice note:</strong> speed, paper and parallel tracks — helpline, portal, bank and FIR together, not in sequence — determine how much of a digital-arrest loss is ever seen again. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
